 "asked_at": "2026-10-17T21:28+08:00",
 "answer": "No, the haze situation in Johor Bahru is roughly comparable to or slightly better than parts of Singapore, though both are experiencing severe, unhealthy-to-hazardous air quality due to regional transboundary smoke. \n\nBecause Johor Bahru and Singapore sit right next to each other across the Straits of Johor, they largely share the same regional weather patterns and smoke drift from fires in Indonesia. Real-time air quality tracking shows:\n* **Johor Bahru** tracking in the \"Very Unhealthy\" AQI range (with specific monitoring stations like Larkin reporting AQI levels around 175 and higher).\n* **Singapore** reporting similar or even higher peaks in certain areas, with parts of the island (such as the West and Central regions) entering the \"Very Unhealthy\" to \"Hazardous\" range with PSI readings pushing past 260–275. \n\nOverall, both sides of the causeway are heavily impacted, and air quality across the southern Malay Peninsula is heavily degraded.",
